{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And at Taberah.</b> It is possible that since it is a place-name and they camped there only one day, therefore it was not mentioned in These are the Journeys. And it is one of three journeys that they traveled from Mount Sinai to Kivrot HaTa'avah, as I explained. And the reason for joining Taberah with Massah is that the people were complaining, as it were testing HaShem, and he mentioned Taberah before Massah because at Massah nothing happened to them. And some say that Amalek came because they tested HaShem, and therefore he mentioned the burning of HaShem's anger against them, and the gnawing of Amalek, and the plague of the complainers — he mentioned the severest first. And some say that Taberah is Massah, like \"from the peak of Snir and Hermon.\" And the correct interpretation is what I explained."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The phrase translated 'the gnawing of Amalek' renders וזנוב עמלק; the word זנוב is unusual and its precise sense here is uncertain, possibly 'attack' or 'pestilence.' The verse citation alluded to, 'from the peak of Snir and Hermon,' is Psalms 42:7. The expression 'in These are the Journeys' refers to Numbers 33."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:22:57.411772805Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}